213
00:19:05,520 --> 00:19:10,399
peculiarities are indiscriminately dispersed among all sorts of whales

214
00:19:10,400 --> 00:19:14,478
without any regard to what may be the nature of their structure and other and

215
00:19:14,479 --> 00:19:19,359
more essential particulars. Thus, the sperm whale and the humpback whale each

216
00:19:19,360 --> 00:19:25,118
has a hump, but there the similitude ceases. Then this same humpback whale

217
00:19:25,119 --> 00:19:30,557
and the Greenland whale, each of these has bene. But there again the similitude

218
00:19:30,558 --> 00:19:36,399
ceases and it is just the same with the other parts above mentioned. In various

219
00:19:36,400 --> 00:19:41,199
sorts of whales they form such irregular combinations or in the case of any one

220
00:19:41,200 --> 00:19:47,278
of them detached such an irregular isolation as utterly to defy all general

221
00:19:47,279 --> 00:19:50,478
methodization formed upon such a basis.

222
00:19:50,479 --> 00:19:56,079
On this rock every one of the whale naturalists has split.

223
00:19:56,080 --> 00:20:01,199
But it may possibly be conceived that in the internal parts of the whale, in his

224
00:20:01,200 --> 00:20:06,636
anatomy, there at least we shall be able to hit the right classification.

225
00:20:06,637 --> 00:20:11,199
Nay, what thing, for example, is there in the Greenland whale's anatomy more

226
00:20:11,200 --> 00:20:16,636
striking than his bene? Yet we have seen that by his bellin it is impossible to

227
00:20:16,637 --> 00:20:19,679
correctly classify the Greenland whale.

228
00:20:19,680 --> 00:20:24,158
And if you descend into the bowels of the various Leviathans, why there you

229
00:20:24,159 --> 00:20:28,956
will not find distinctions a 50th part as available to the systematizer as

230
00:20:28,957 --> 00:20:31,839
those external ones already enumerated.

231
00:20:31,840 --> 00:20:36,877
What then remains nothing but to take hold of the whales bodily in their

232
00:20:36,878 --> 00:20:42,478
entire liberal volume and boldly sort them that way. And this is the

233
00:20:42,479 --> 00:20:47,519
bibliographical system here adopted and it is the only one that can possibly

234
00:20:47,520 --> 00:20:50,796
succeed for it alone is practicable.

235
00:20:50,797 --> 00:20:58,877
To proceed book one folio chapter 4 humpback. This whale is often seen on

236
00:20:58,878 --> 00:21:03,999
the northern American coast. He has been frequently captured there and towed into

237
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:09,118
harbor. He has a great pack on him like a peddler or you might call him the

238
00:21:09,119 --> 00:21:11,438
elephant and castle whale.

239
00:21:11,439 --> 00:21:15,839
At any rate, the popular name for him does not sufficiently distinguish him

240
00:21:15,840 --> 00:21:20,877
since the sperm whale also has a hump, though a smaller one. His oil is not

241
00:21:20,878 --> 00:21:25,758
very valuable. He has baileine. He is the most gamesome and light-hearted of

242
00:21:25,759 --> 00:21:32,557
all whales, making more gay foam and white water generally than any other of them.

243
00:21:32,558 --> 00:21:36,796
Book one, Folio. Chapter 5. Razerback.

244
00:21:36,797 --> 00:21:41,758
Of this whale, little is known but his name. I have seen him at a distance off

245
00:21:41,759 --> 00:21:48,239
Cape Horn. Of a retiring nature, he eludes both hunters and philosophers.

246
00:21:48,240 --> 00:21:52,956
Though no coward, he has never yet shown any part of him but his back, which

247
00:21:52,957 --> 00:21:58,956
rises in a long, sharp ridge. Let him go. I know little more of him, and nor

248
00:21:58,957 --> 00:22:02,636
does anybody else. Book one. Folio.

249
00:22:02,637 --> 00:22:08,158
Chapter 6. Sulfurbottom. Another retiring gentleman with a brimstone

250
00:22:08,159 --> 00:22:13,359
belly, doubtless got by scraping along the Tartarian tiles in some of his

251
00:22:13,360 --> 00:22:18,717
profounder divings. He is seldom seen, at least I have never seen him, except

252
00:22:18,718 --> 00:22:23,679
in the remoter southern seas, and then always at too great a distance to study

253
00:22:23,680 --> 00:22:29,278
his countenance. He is never chased. He would run away with rope walks of line.

254
00:22:29,279 --> 00:22:33,839
Prodigies are told of him. Adue, sulfurbottom, I can say nothing more

255
00:22:33,840 --> 00:22:38,158
that is true of ye, nor can the oldest Nantucketer.

256
00:22:38,159 --> 00:22:43,999
Thus ends book one, Folio, and now begins book two, Octavo.

257
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:45,918
Octavos.

258
00:22:45,919 --> 00:22:50,956
These embrace the whales of middling magnitude among which present may be

259
00:22:50,957 --> 00:22:57,918
numbered one the grampus, two the blackfish, three the narwhale, four the

260
00:22:57,919 --> 00:23:01,438
thrasher, five the killer.

261
00:23:01,439 --> 00:23:07,918
Footnote. Why this book of whales is not denominated the quarto is very plain.

262
00:23:07,919 --> 00:23:11,999
Because while the whales of this order, those smaller than those of the former

263
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:16,079
order, nevertheless retain a proportionate likeness to them in

264
00:23:16,080 --> 00:23:21,359
figure, yet the bookbinder's quorto volume, in its dimensioned form, does

265
00:23:21,360 --> 00:23:29,519
not preserve the shape of the folio volume, but the octavo volume does. End of footnote.

266
00:23:29,520 --> 00:23:33,918
Book two, octavo, chapter 1, grandpas.

267
00:23:33,919 --> 00:23:38,318
Though this fish, whose loud sonorous breathing, or rather blowing, has

268
00:23:38,319 --> 00:23:43,839
furnished a proverb to landsmen, is so well known a denisin of the deep, yet he

269
00:23:43,840 --> 00:23:47,039
is not popularly classed among whales.

270
00:23:47,040 --> 00:23:51,199
But possessing all the grand distinctive features of the Leviathan, most

271
00:23:51,200 --> 00:23:53,839
naturalists have recognized him for one.

272
00:23:53,840 --> 00:23:59,999
He is of moderate octavo size, varying from 15 to 25 ft in length, and of

273
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:04,956
corresponding dimensions round the waist. He swims in herds. He is never

274
00:24:04,957 --> 00:24:09,519
regularly hunted, though his oil is considerable in quantity and pretty good

275
00:24:09,520 --> 00:24:14,636
for light. By some fisherman, his approach is regarded as premonatory of

276
00:24:14,637 --> 00:24:18,158
the advance of the great sperm whale.

277
00:24:18,159 --> 00:24:22,557
Book two, octavo, chapter 2, blackfish.

278
00:24:22,558 --> 00:24:27,359
I give the popular fisherman's names for all these fish, for generally they are

279
00:24:27,360 --> 00:24:32,636
best. Where any name happens to be vague or inexpressive, I shall say so and

280
00:24:32,637 --> 00:24:38,079
suggest another. I do so now, touching the blackfish, socalled, because

281
00:24:38,080 --> 00:24:43,598
blackness is the rule among almost all whales. So call him the hyena whale, if

282
00:24:43,599 --> 00:24:48,796
you please. His boracity is well known, and from the circumstance that the inner

283
00:24:48,797 --> 00:24:54,557
angles of his lips are curved upwards, he carries an everlasting mephilian grin

284
00:24:54,558 --> 00:25:00,796
on his face. This whale averages some 16 or 18 feet in length. He is found in

285
00:25:00,797 --> 00:25:05,999
almost all latitudes. He has a peculiar way of showing his dorsal hooked fin in

286
00:25:06,000 --> 00:25:11,118
swimming, which looks something like a Roman nose. When not more profitably

287
00:25:11,119 --> 00:25:15,679
employed, these sperm whale hunters sometimes capture the hyena whale to

288
00:25:15,680 --> 00:25:20,239
keep up the supply of cheap oil for domestic employment, as some frugal

289
00:25:20,240 --> 00:25:24,636
housekeepers, in the absence of company, and quite alone by themselves, burn

290
00:25:24,637 --> 00:25:28,079
unsaavory tallow instead of odorous wax.

291
00:25:28,080 --> 00:25:32,239
Though their blubber is very thin, some of these whales will yield you upwards

292
00:25:32,240 --> 00:25:35,039
of 30 gallons of oil.

293
00:25:35,040 --> 00:25:38,877
Book two, octavo, chapter 3, narwhale.

294
00:25:38,878 --> 00:25:45,839
that is nostril whale. Another instance of a curiously named whale, so named, I

295
00:25:45,840 --> 00:25:51,199
suppose, from his peculiar horn being originally mistaken for a peaked nose.

296
00:25:51,200 --> 00:25:56,717
The creature is some 16 ft in length, while its horn averages 5 ft, though

297
00:25:56,718 --> 00:26:00,478
some exceed 10 and even attain to 15 ft.

298
00:26:00,479 --> 00:26:05,199
Strictly speaking, this horn is but a lengthened tusk growing out from the jaw

299
00:26:05,200 --> 00:26:10,478
in a line a little depressed from the horizontal, but it is only found on the

300
00:26:10,479 --> 00:26:15,199
sinister side, which has an ill effect, giving its owner something analogous to

301
00:26:15,200 --> 00:26:18,717
the aspect of a clumsy left-handed man.

302
00:26:18,718 --> 00:26:24,239
What precise purpose this ivory horn or lance answers, it would be hard to say.

303
00:26:24,240 --> 00:26:28,717
It does not seem to be used like the blade of the swordfish and billfish,

304
00:26:28,718 --> 00:26:32,956
though some sailors tell me that the narwhale employs it for a rake in

305
00:26:32,957 --> 00:26:37,598
turning over the bottom of the sea for food. Charlie Coffan said it was used

306
00:26:37,599 --> 00:26:42,717
for an ice piercer, for the narwhale, rising to the surface of the polar sea,

307
00:26:42,718 --> 00:26:46,877
and finding it sheetated with ice thrusts his horn up and so breaks

308
00:26:46,878 --> 00:26:52,239
through. But you cannot prove either of these surmises to be correct. My own

309
00:26:52,240 --> 00:26:57,679
opinion is that however this one-sided horn may really be used by the narwhale,

310
00:26:57,680 --> 00:27:02,796
however that may be, it would certainly be very convenient to him for a folder

311
00:27:02,797 --> 00:27:07,918
in reading pamphlets. The narwhale I have heard called the tusked whale, the

312
00:27:07,919 --> 00:27:13,598
horned whale, and the unicorn whale. He is certainly a curious example of the

313
00:27:13,599 --> 00:27:19,118
unicornism to be found in almost every kingdom of animated nature. From certain

314
00:27:19,119 --> 00:27:24,399
cloistered old authors, I have gathered that this same sea unicorn's horn was in

315
00:27:24,400 --> 00:27:29,039
ancient days regarded as the great antidote against poison, and as such

316
00:27:29,040 --> 00:27:33,679
preparations of it brought immense prices. It was also distilled to a

317
00:27:33,680 --> 00:27:38,478
volatile salts for fainting ladies, the same way that the horns of the male deer

318
00:27:38,479 --> 00:27:41,199
are manufactured into heartsorn.

319
00:27:41,200 --> 00:27:46,399
Originally, it was in itself accounted an object of great curiosity. Black

320
00:27:46,400 --> 00:27:50,636
letter tells me that Sir Martin Frobisher, on his return from that

321
00:27:50,637 --> 00:27:54,956
voyage, when Queen Best did gallantly wave her jeweled hand to him from a

322
00:27:54,957 --> 00:28:00,079
window of Greenwich Palace as his bold ship sailed down the tempames, quote,

323
00:28:00,080 --> 00:28:04,877
"When Sir Martin returned from that voyage," Seth Blacklet Letter, on bended

324
00:28:04,878 --> 00:28:09,438
knees, he presented to her highness a prodigious long horn of the Narwhale,

325
00:28:09,439 --> 00:28:14,636
which for a long period after hung in the castle at Windsor. End quote. an

326
00:28:14,637 --> 00:28:19,519
Irish author of hers that the Earl of Leester on bended knees did likewise

327
00:28:19,520 --> 00:28:25,999
present to her highness another horn pertaining to a land beast of the unicorn nature.

328
00:28:26,000 --> 00:28:31,598
The narwhale has a very picturesque leopardike look being of a milk white

329
00:28:31,599 --> 00:28:37,199
ground color dotted with round and oblong spots of black. His oil is very

330
00:28:37,200 --> 00:28:42,478
superior, clean and fine, but there is little of it and he is seldom hunted. He

331
00:28:42,479 --> 00:28:46,158
is mostly found in these circumpolar seas.

332
00:28:46,159 --> 00:28:52,318
Book two. Octavo. Chapter 4. Killer. Of this whale. Little is precisely known to

333
00:28:52,319 --> 00:28:57,278
the Nantucketer, and nothing at all to the professed naturalist. From what I

334
00:28:57,279 --> 00:29:01,199
have seen of him at a distance, I should say he was about the bigness of a

335
00:29:01,200 --> 00:29:06,956
grampus. He is very savage, a sort of Fiji fish. He sometimes takes the great

336
00:29:06,957 --> 00:29:11,438
folio whales by the lip and hangs there like a leech till the mighty brood is

337
00:29:11,439 --> 00:29:16,239
worried to death. The killer is never hunted. I never heard what sort of oil

338
00:29:16,240 --> 00:29:21,039
he has. Exception might be taken to the name bestowed upon this whale on the

339
00:29:21,040 --> 00:29:26,239
ground of its indistinctness. For we are all killers on land and on sea,

340
00:29:26,240 --> 00:29:29,519
bonaparts and sharks included.

341
00:29:29,520 --> 00:29:33,839
Book two. Octavo. Chapter 5. Thrasher.

342
00:29:33,840 --> 00:29:38,796
This gentleman is famous for his tail, which he uses for a feral in thrashing

343
00:29:38,797 --> 00:29:43,918
his foes. He mounts the folio whales back, and as he swims, he works his

344
00:29:43,919 --> 00:29:48,636
passage by flogging him, as some school masters get along in the world by a

345
00:29:48,637 --> 00:29:53,519
similar process. Still less is known of the thrasher than of the killer. Both

346
00:29:53,520 --> 00:29:57,519
are outlaws, even in the lawless seas.

347
00:29:57,520 --> 00:30:03,679
Thus ends book two, Octavo, and begins book three, Duesimo.

348
00:30:03,680 --> 00:30:05,278
duodesimos.

349
00:30:05,279 --> 00:30:11,039
These include the smaller whales. One, the huzzah porpus. Two, the alggerine

350
00:30:11,040 --> 00:30:17,199
porpus. Three, the mely mouth porpus. To those who have not chance specifically

351
00:30:17,200 --> 00:30:22,239
to study the subject, it may possibly seem strange that fishes not commonly

352
00:30:22,240 --> 00:30:27,359
exceeding four or 5 ft should be marshaled among whales, a word which in

353
00:30:27,360 --> 00:30:32,399
the popular sense always conveys the idea of huggeeness. But the creatures

354
00:30:32,400 --> 00:30:37,278
set down above as duodesimos are infallibly whales by the terms of my

355
00:30:37,279 --> 00:30:44,079
definition of what a whale is, i.e. a spouting fish with a horizontal tail.

356
00:30:44,080 --> 00:30:50,877
Book three, duodeimo chapter 1. Huzzah porpus. This is the common porpus found

357
00:30:50,878 --> 00:30:55,918
almost all over the globe. The name is of my own bestowyl, for there are more

358
00:30:55,919 --> 00:31:00,717
than one sort of porposes, and something must be done to distinguish them. I call

359
00:31:00,718 --> 00:31:05,918
him thus because he always swims in hilarious schos which upon the broad sea

360
00:31:05,919 --> 00:31:11,039
keep tossing themselves to heaven like caps in a Fourth of July crowd. Their

361
00:31:11,040 --> 00:31:15,438
appearances generally hailed with delight by the mariner. Full of fine

362
00:31:15,439 --> 00:31:20,399
spirits. They invariably come from the breezy billows to windward. They are the

363
00:31:20,400 --> 00:31:23,278
lads that always live before the wind.

364
00:31:23,279 --> 00:31:28,318
They are accounted a lucky omen. If you yourself can withstand three cheers at

365
00:31:28,319 --> 00:31:33,039
beholding these vivacious fish, then heaven help you. The spirit of godly

366
00:31:33,040 --> 00:31:38,796
gamesomeness is not in you. A well-fed, plump huzah porpus will yield you one

367
00:31:38,797 --> 00:31:43,758
good gallon of good oil, but the fine and delicate fluid extracted from his

368
00:31:43,759 --> 00:31:49,438
jaws is exceedingly valuable. It is in request among jewelers and watch makers.

369
00:31:49,439 --> 00:31:54,557
Sailors put it on their hones. Porpus meat is good eating, you know. It may

370
00:31:54,558 --> 00:31:58,877
never have occurred to you that a porpus spouts. Indeed, his spout is so small

371
00:31:58,878 --> 00:32:03,359
that it is not very readily discernible, but the next time you have a chance,

372
00:32:03,360 --> 00:32:09,598
watch him, and you will then see the great sperm whale himself in miniature.

373
00:32:09,599 --> 00:32:17,118
Book three, Duodesimo. Chapter 2, Algerine Corpus, a pirate, very savage.

374
00:32:17,119 --> 00:32:21,359
He is only found, I think, in the Pacific. He is somewhat larger than the

375
00:32:21,360 --> 00:32:26,318
Huzah porpus, but much of the same general make. Provoke him and he will

376
00:32:26,319 --> 00:32:33,278
buckle to a shark. I have lowered for him many times, but never yet saw him captured.

377
00:32:33,279 --> 00:32:36,399
Book three, Duodesimo. Chapter 3.

378
00:32:36,400 --> 00:32:41,679
Meymouth porpus. The largest kind of porpus, and only found in the Pacific,

379
00:32:41,680 --> 00:32:46,399
so far as it is known. The only English name by which he has hitherto been

380
00:32:46,400 --> 00:32:50,877
designated is that of the fissurers right whale porpus from the circumstance

381
00:32:50,878 --> 00:32:56,399
that he is chiefly found in the vicinity of that folio. In shape he differs in

382
00:32:56,400 --> 00:33:02,079
some degree from the huzah porpus being of a less rotund and jolly girth. Indeed

383
00:33:02,080 --> 00:33:07,438
he is of quite a neat and gentlemanlike figure. He has no fins on his back most

384
00:33:07,439 --> 00:33:12,796
other porpuses have. He has a lovely tail and sentimental Indian eyes of a

385
00:33:12,797 --> 00:33:19,278
hazel hue, but his mely mouth spoils all. Though his entire back down to his

386
00:33:19,279 --> 00:33:24,717
side fins, is of a deep sable, yet a boundary line distinct as the mark in a

387
00:33:24,718 --> 00:33:29,598
ship's hull, called the bright waist, that line streaks him from stem to

388
00:33:29,599 --> 00:33:35,039
stern, with two separate colors, black above and white below. The white

389
00:33:35,040 --> 00:33:39,598
comprises part of his head and the whole of his mouth, which makes him look as if

390
00:33:39,599 --> 00:33:44,636
he had just escaped from a felonious visit to a meal bag, a most mean and

391
00:33:44,637 --> 00:33:50,478
mely aspect. His oil is much like that of the common porpus.

392
00:33:50,479 --> 00:33:55,839
Beyond the duesimo, this system does not proceed in as much as the porpus is the

393
00:33:55,840 --> 00:34:00,877
smallest of the whales. Above you have all the Leviathans of note, but there

394
00:34:00,878 --> 00:34:05,598
are a rabble of uncertain, fugitive, half-fabulous whales, which as an

395
00:34:05,599 --> 00:34:10,796
American whaleman I know by reputation, but not personally. I shall enumerate

396
00:34:10,797 --> 00:34:16,079
them by their folks appellations, for possibly such a list may be valuable to

397
00:34:16,080 --> 00:34:21,199
future investigators who may complete what I have here but begun. If any of

398
00:34:21,200 --> 00:34:25,199
the following whales shall hereafter be caught and marked, then he can readily

399
00:34:25,200 --> 00:34:30,478
be incorporated into this system according to his folio octavo or duode

400
00:34:30,479 --> 00:34:36,478
decimo magnitude. The bottl-nose whale, the junk whale, the puddingheaded whale,

401
00:34:36,479 --> 00:34:42,717
the cape whale, the leading whale, the cannon whale, the scrag whale, the

402
00:34:42,718 --> 00:34:48,478
coppered whale, the elephant whale, the iceberg whale, the quag whale, the blue

403
00:34:48,479 --> 00:34:54,717
whale, etc. From Icelandic, Dutch, and old English authorities, there might be

404
00:34:54,718 --> 00:34:59,438
quoted other lists of uncertain whales blessed with all manner of uncouthed

405
00:34:59,439 --> 00:35:04,636
names, but I omit them as altogether obsolete, and can hardly help suspecting

406
00:35:04,637 --> 00:35:10,636
them for mere sounds full of Leviathanism, but signifying nothing.

407
00:35:10,637 --> 00:35:15,519
Finally, it was stated at the outset that this system would not be here, and

408
00:35:15,520 --> 00:35:20,717
at once perfected. You cannot but plainly see that I have kept my word.

409
00:35:20,718 --> 00:35:25,519
But I now leave my cological system standing thus unfinished, even as the

410
00:35:25,520 --> 00:35:30,239
great cathedral of Cologne was left with the crane still standing upon the top of

411
00:35:30,240 --> 00:35:35,598
the uncompleted tower. For small erections may be finished by their first

412
00:35:35,599 --> 00:35:42,399
architects. Grand ones, true ones, ever leave the copstone to posterity. God

413
00:35:42,400 --> 00:35:45,199
keep me from ever completing anything.

414
00:35:45,200 --> 00:35:51,199
This whole book is but a draft. Nay, but the draft of a draft. Oh, time,

415
00:35:51,200 --> 00:35:56,520
strength, cash, and patience.
